<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-colour">
  <title>Colour</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "I found I could say things with color and shapes that I couldn't say any other way - things I had no words
        for."
      </p>
      <p>--- Georgia O'Keeffe</p>
    </blockquote>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-colour-theory">
    <title>Theory and implementation</title>

    <p>
      Colour is technically complex and supported by many representation systems and palette families. Modern software
      offers qualitative, sequential, diverging, and colour-blind-aware palettes, but defaults are not always
      suitable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-colour-perception">
    <title>Perception of colour</title>

    <p>
      Colour perception depends on observer differences, ambient conditions, display medium, and neighbouring colours.
      This variability means colour choices should be robust rather than merely aesthetically pleasing.
    </p>
  </section>

