    <subsection xml:id="sec-colour-attract">
      <title>Colours attract</title>
      <p>
        Well-chosen colour can improve engagement and retention, but should remain purposeful and restrained to avoid
        visual clutter.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-colour-choice">
    <title>Choice of colour</title>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-colour-blind">
      <title>Colour blind palettes help</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-colblind-1">
        <image source="images/colBlind-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Example 1 using a colour-blind-friendly palette.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="fig-colblind-2">
        <image source="images/colBlind-2.png"/>
        <caption><p>Example 2 using a colour-blind-friendly palette.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="fig-colblind-3">
        <image source="images/colBlind-3.png"/>
        <caption><p>Example 3 using a colour-blind-friendly palette.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-colour-order">
      <title>Palette order counts</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-astro4">
        <image source="images/astro4-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Spaceflight mission times with reordered palette for clarity.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-colour-not-enough">
      <title>There may not be enough colours</title>
      <p>
        When categories are numerous, direct one-colour-per-category mapping fails; aggregation, faceting, or
        alternative encodings become necessary.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-colour-avoid-reuse">
      <title>Avoid using the same colours for different datasets</title>
      <p>
        Reusing the same palette across unrelated contexts can imply false equivalence. Colour semantics should remain
        context-specific.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-colour-overlap-faceting">
      <title>Avoid overlapping colours, use faceting</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-finchnine">
        <image source="images/finchNine-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Faceted finch display with ghosted context for readability.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-colour-backgrounds">
      <title>Backgrounds matter</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-backs-1">
        <image source="images/backs-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Graphic example with plain background 1.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="fig-backs-2">
        <image source="images/backs-2.png"/>
        <caption><p>Graphic example with plain background 2.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="fig-backs-3">
        <image source="images/backs-3.png"/>
        <caption><p>Graphic example with plain background 3.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Surrounding design context changes colour perception; background, medium, and display conditions should be
        considered when finalizing a palette.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-colour-main-points">
      <title>Main points</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Colour systems and palettes are numerous; colour choice is nontrivial.</p></li>
        <li><p>Use colour intentionally: distinguish, highlight, connect, separate, signify.</p></li>
        <li><p>Effective choices require awareness of perceptual differences and context.</p></li>
        <li><p>Ghostplotting and faceting are valuable when overlap reduces readability.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
